Certainly, here is an example of a PHP script that reads values from a database and displays them on a graph using the PHPExcel library:

```
<?php

// Connect to the database
$conn = mysqli_connect("host", "username", "password", "database");

// Retrieve data from the database
$sql = "SELECT * FROM data_table";
$result = mysqli_query($conn, $sql);

// Read values from the database
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result)) {
  $x = $row["x"];
  $y = $row["y"];
}

// Close the database connection
mysqli_close($conn);

// Include the PHPExcel library
require_once 'PHPExcel.php';

// Create a new PHPExcel object
$objPHPExcel = new PHPExcel();

// Add a new worksheet to the workbook
$objPHPExcel->addSheet();

// Write the values to the worksheet
for ($i = 0; $i < count($x); $i++) {
  $objPHPExcel->getActiveSheet()->setCellValue("A$i", $x[$i]);
  $objPHPExcel->getActiveSheet()->setCellValue("B$i", $y[$i]);
}

// Save the workbook as a.xls file
$objWriter = PHPExcel_IOFactory::createWriter($objPHPExcel, 'Excel2007');
$objWriter->save('data.xls');

?>
```

This script assumes that you have a database with a table named "data\_table" that has two columns named "x" and "y" that contain the values you want to display on the graph. The script uses the PHPExcel library to create a new workbook and sheet, and then writes the values to the sheet in the format A1, B1, A2, B2, etc. You can customize this script to suit your specific needs and requirements.
